Ostwald's process :-

4 \mathrm{NH}_3(\mathrm{~g})+5 \mathrm{O}_2(\mathrm{~g})
\stackrel{\text { Pt/Rh gauge catalyst }}{500 \mathrm{~K}, 9 \text { bar }}
$4 \mathrm{NO}(\mathrm{g})+6 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}(\mathrm{g})$
Lead Chamber process:

\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{NO}+\frac{1}{2} \mathrm{O}_2 \rightarrow \mathrm{NO}_2 \\
& \mathrm{NO}_2+\mathrm{SO}_2 \rightarrow \mathrm{NO}+\mathrm{SO}_3 ; \mathrm{SO}_3+\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O} \rightarrow \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{SO}_4
\end{aligned}

Deacon's process :

4 \mathrm{HCl}+\mathrm{O}_2 \underset{723 \mathrm{~K}}{\stackrel{\mathrm{CuCl}_2}{\longrightarrow}} 2 \mathrm{Cl}_2+2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}

Haber's process :
$\mathrm{N}_2+3 \mathrm{H}_2 \underset{\mathrm{Fe}}{\stackrel{250 \mathrm{~atm}}{\rightleftharpoons}} 2 \mathrm{NH}_3$

When chlorine react with water, it gives HCl and oxygen.
$\underset{\text { Chlorine }}{\mathrm{Cl}_2}+\underset{\text { Water }}{\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}} \longrightarrow 2 \mathrm{HCl}+[\mathrm{O}]$

While other reactions take place as follows :

$\underset{\text { Sodium thiosulphate }}{\mathrm{Na}_2 \mathrm{~S}_2 \mathrm{O}_3(a q)}+\mathrm{Cl}_2+\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O} \longrightarrow\underset{\text { Sulphur }}{\mathrm{S}}+\underset{\text { Sodium sulphate }}{\mathrm{Na}_2 \mathrm{SO}_4}+2 \mathrm{HCl}\underset{\substack{\text { Sodium } \\ \text { hydroxide }}}{\mathrm{NaOH}}+\mathrm{Cl}_2 \longrightarrow \underset{\substack{\text { Sodium } \\ \text { chloride }}}{\mathrm{NaCl}}+\underset{\begin{array}{c}\text { Sodium } \\ \text { hypochlorite }\end{array}}{\mathrm{NaClO}}+\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}$
\underset{\begin{array}{l}
\text { Calcium } \\
\text { hydroxide }
\end{array}}{\mathrm{Ca}(\mathrm{OH})_2}+\mathrm{Cl}_2 \longrightarrow \underset{\substack{\text { Calcium } \\
\text { oxychloride }}}{\mathrm{CaOCl}_2}+\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}

Both magnesium $(\mathrm{Mg})$ and manganese $(\mathrm{Mn})$ react with very dilute cold $\mathrm{HNO}_3$ to evolve $\mathrm{H}_2$ gas. Magnesium metal reacts with dilute nitric acid to form magnesium nitrate and hydrogen gas

\mathrm{Mg}+\underset{\begin{array}{c}
\text { Cold and } \\
\text { dilute }
\end{array}}{2 \mathrm{HNO}_3} \longrightarrow \underset{\begin{array}{c}
\text { Magnesium } \\
\text { nitrate }
\end{array}}{\mathrm{Mg}\left(\mathrm{NO}_3\right)_2}+\mathrm{H}_2(g)

Hydrogen gas is not evolved when other metals react with nitric acid because it is a strong oxidising agents. It oxidises hydrogen produced to water and itself gets reduced to nitrogen oxide.

Nitrous acid was disproportionated to form water, $\mathrm{HNO}_3$ and $\mathrm{NO}$. In another reaction, sodium nitrite was reacted with $\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{SO}_4$ to form $\mathrm{NaHSO}_4$, $\mathrm{HNO}_3$, water and NO.

\begin{aligned}
& 3 \mathrm{HNO}_2 \longrightarrow \mathrm{HNO}_3+\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}+2 \mathrm{NO} \\
& 2 \mathrm{NaNO}_2+\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{SO}_4 \longrightarrow \mathrm{NaHSO}_4+\mathrm{HNO}_3+\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}+\mathrm{NO}
\end{aligned}

\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{C}+4 \mathrm{HNO}_3 \longrightarrow \mathrm{CO}_2+4 \mathrm{NO}_2+2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O} \\
& \mathrm{C}+2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{SO}_4 \longrightarrow \mathrm{CO}_2+2 \mathrm{SO}_2+2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}
\end{aligned}

$\mathrm{CO}_2$ and $\mathrm{SO}_2$ both are acidic.

Sodium thiosulphate is oxidised by moist $\mathrm{Cl}_2$ or chlorine water and precipitates sulphur,

\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{Na}_2 \mathrm{~S}_2 \mathrm{O}_3+\mathrm{Cl}_2+\mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O} \longrightarrow \mathrm{Na}_2 \mathrm{SO}_4+2 \mathrm{HCl} \\
& \text { sodium } \\
& \text { thiosulphate }
\end{aligned}
